.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii registercloudguest.1
.\"
.TH REGISTERCLOUDGUEST 1 "" "cloud-regionsrv-client" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
registercloudguest \- Register a SUSE Linux Enterprise instance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B registercloudguest
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B registercloudguest
\fB\-r\fR \fIregcode\fR [\fB\-e\fR \fIemail\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B registercloudguest \-\-clean
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B registercloudguest
registers a SUSE Linux Enterprise instance with the closest, in most
cases region local, SUSE operated update infrastructure. The client reaches
out to a so called region server to obtain information about the closest
update servers. The information about how to find any one of the region
servers running in a given cloud framework is built into the image that
contains the registercloudguest executable. The information of the update
servers is cached to aid with HA fail-over if one of the update servers is
unreachable. The system is registered with the first responsive update server.

None of the region and update servers are resolvable via DNS and the update
server name resolution is enforced via an entry in /etc/hosts.

For on-demand (PAYG) instances users should not modify the default repository
configuration. It is highly likely that something on the system will break
if repositories are removed using SUSEConnect functionality. The addition
of other repositories is supported. However, adding other repositories may
cause problems when the migration path for a new service pack or distribution
upgrade is calculated. Additional repositories may have to be removed prior to
migration. The command line tool should only be used in exceptional
circumstances and to activate Long Term Service Pack Support (LTSS), see
.BR "LTSS REGISTRATION" .

For BYOS instances the initial registration has to be completed using the
registercloudguest command with the
.B \-\-regcode
option. Registering additional products (repositories) is done using the
SUSEConnect command as it would be used in a data center installation of a
SUSE Linux Enterprise system. LTSS is the exception, it is activated with
registercloudguest, see
.BR "LTSS REGISTRATION" .
BYOS instances registered against the update infrastructure are counted in
the SUSE Customer Center (SCC) in the subscription associated with the
provided registration code. The instance is visible in the SCC UI. The command
line tool should only be used for the initial registration of the system, the
activation of LTSS, and the de-registration of the system.
.SH LTSS REGISTRATION
The meaning of the
.B \-r
option depends on the registration state of the system:
.IP \(bu 2
On a system that is not registered, the registration code is used to register
the base product. This is how BYOS instances are registered.
.IP \(bu 2
On a system that is already registered, PAYG or BYOS, the registration code is
used to activate LTSS. The LTSS product is always registered as SLES-LTSS,
also for variants of SUSE Linux Enterprise Server such as SLES for SAP
Applications or SLE HPC. If LTSS is already active, nothing changes.
.PP
LTSS can therefore only be activated after the base product is registered.
Running
.B registercloudguest \-r
with an LTSS registration code on a system that is not registered fails.
.PP
Running the base registration again on a registered system, for example
after a timeout, does not register the base product again. Instead, the
registration code is used for an LTSS activation, which fails for a
registration code of the base product. To register a registered system
again, use
.B \-\-force\-new
together with
.BR \-r ,
or run
.B registercloudguest \-\-clean
first.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-clean
Clean the running instance of any data related to repository setup and
instance registration. Use this option when creating new images from
running instances before taking the snapshot of the root volume. For BYOS
instances registered against the update infrastructure the system will be
removed from SCC. This option and
.B \-\-force\-new
are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delay " \fIseconds\fR"
Delay the start of the registration by the given number of seconds.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Debug mode, increase verbosity of logfile information.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-email " \fIemail\fR"
The e-mail address associated with a subscription in SCC. This option
requires the
.B \-\-regcode
option.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-config\-file " \fIfile\fR"
Specify the location of the configuration file. By default the configuration
file /etc/regionserverclnt.cfg is expected and used.
.TP
.B \-\-force\-new
Force a new registration. The previous registration data is removed, for
BYOS instances registered against the update infrastructure this includes
the removal of the system from SCC, and the system is registered again with
the options of this call as if it had never been registered. For BYOS
instances, provide the registration code with
.B \-\-regcode
again. This is an "emergency" switch and should be used only in exceptional
circumstances. This option and
.B \-\-clean
are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-regcode " \fIregcode\fR"
A registration code associated with a SUSE subscription, which is validated
with SCC. On a system that is not registered, the system is registered with
the code and associated with its SCC subscription, as required for BYOS
instances. On a system that is already registered, PAYG or BYOS, the code is
used to activate LTSS, see
.BR "LTSS REGISTRATION" .
.TP
.BI \-\-smt\-fp " fingerprint"
Specify the fingerprint of the update server certificate. This option must be
used in conjunction with
.B \-\-smt\-fqdn
and
.BR \-\-smt\-ip .
.TP
.BI \-\-smt\-fqdn " fqdn"
Specify the update server fully qualified domain name. This option must be
used in conjunction with
.B \-\-smt\-fp
and
.BR \-\-smt\-ip .
.TP
.BI \-\-smt\-ip " ip"
Specify the update server IPv4 or IPv6 address. This option must be used
in conjunction with
.B \-\-smt\-fp
and
.BR \-\-smt\-fqdn .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a help message and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Register a BYOS instance:
.PP
.RS
.B registercloudguest \-r
.I BASE_REGCODE
.RE
.PP
Activate LTSS on a registered PAYG or BYOS instance:
.PP
.RS
.B registercloudguest \-r
.I LTSS_REGCODE
.RE
.PP
Register a registered BYOS instance again:
.PP
.RS
.B registercloudguest \-\-force\-new \-r
.I BASE_REGCODE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/regionserverclnt.cfg
The default configuration file.
.TP
.I /var/log/cloudregister
The log file.
.TP
.I /var/cache/cloudregister
The cached registration and update server data.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR SUSEConnect (8)
.SH AUTHOR
SUSE Public Cloud Team (public-cloud-dev@susecloud.net)
